  "statement": "Safety advocates make a serious argument for physical controls. They argue that tracking massive data centers and clusters exceeding 100,000 chips prevents runaway catastrophic risks without harming beneficial tools like medical software. That distinction sounds neat on paper, but it collapses in practice. Modern biology does not run on tiny, isolated calculators. Breakthroughs like automated molecular design require massive foundation models—general systems trained on vast data patterns across multiple scientific disciplines. Capping computer chips at arbitrary physical thresholds does not surgically stop dangerous agents. It starves the foundational computing clusters required to crack complex protein dynamics and design life-saving therapies.\n\nPhysical meltdowns in nuclear plants cannot be fixed after an explosion. That danger is real for tightly coupled physical systems. Software is fundamentally different. Inside large neural networks, we can trace internal circuits, map active concepts, and measure safety continuously during training runs. We can enforce rigorous pre-deployment auditing and strict legal liability for actual harms rather than cutting power lines to data centers. Texas adopted this practical logic in legislative bill analysis \"capitol.texas.gov tlodocs 89R analysis html HB00149S.htm\", limiting state lawsuits to deployed systems rather than penalizing unfinished technology. Accountability belongs where software touches the real world, not at electrical substations.\n\nFreezing progress does not create safety. It creates guaranteed casualties. Every month delayed on frontier computing clusters means more patients dying from incurable cancers, Alzheimer's, and antibiotic-resistant infections. As reported in \"Eight ways AI will shape geopolitics in 2026\", national initiatives like the Stargate project plan $500 billion in infrastructure over five years because compute power drives discovery. Pausing computing power at an arbitrary line locks in ongoing human misery to prevent hypothetical whiteboard scenarios. If you vote to freeze this infrastructure, you must explain to sick families why theoretical fears outweigh their actual lives today.",
