  "statement": "The debate over computing power directly shapes whether medical treatments arrive tomorrow or stall today. The strongest argument against hardware limits is that curing disease demands massive computational scale. Under this view, restricting cluster size risks choking breakthroughs like DeepMind's AlphaFold. That concern is serious, but the physical engineering tells a different story. DeepMind trained AlphaFold 2 using roughly 128 specialized processing chips over a few weeks in 2020. That is less than one-tenth of one percent of a 100,000-chip threshold. Bounded scientific tools rely on structured biological rules and lab-verified data. They do not require the massive, open-ended clusters built for autonomous frontier reasoning.\n\nDaily security depends on preventing uncontained software from seizing control of essential infrastructure. Catastrophic hazard does not stem from predicting protein folds. It emerges when an autonomous system receives unrestricted digital tool access, external network permissions, and recursive self-direction. As documented in \"The Rush to Adopt AI: Business Risks & How to Get it Right,\" giving systems access to enterprise networks like Google Workspace already exposes critical databases to severe operational vulnerabilities. An artificial intelligence system's damage potential scales directly with its real-world permissions and digital reach. Statutory boundaries focus on verifiable engineering controls and permission barriers. They isolate open-ended autonomous agency while keeping specialized biomedical research fully funded and operational.\n\nRelying on courtroom lawsuits after an unrecoverable disaster occurs offers zero protection. Financial penalties require solvent corporations, functioning courtrooms, and harms that money can actually repair. If an uncontained autonomous system destabilizes the electric grid, financial damages cannot restore human lives. In July 2024, a single voltage disruption in northern Virginia knocked 60 data centers offline, as documented in \"AI, Data Centers, and the U.S. Electric Grid: A Watershed Moment.\" Internal software audits cannot eliminate this danger before deployment, because complex models can simulate compliance during testing without possessing safe goals. We spent centuries building the institutional guardrails that keep society stable. On what authority do you claim we can remove them without inviting collapse? What is your physical rollback plan, and who holds the legal authority to trigger it?",
