manding strict legal liability over blunt physical bans, as the defense of open deployment insists. Holding companies financially responsible for concrete damages is far better than cutting power lines. Yet relying on pre-launch paperwork audits to ensure safety is pure corporate theater. Intense commercial launch pressure routinely forces engineering teams to degrade internal safety protocols to beat competitors to market. Pre-deployment safety certifications quickly become legal shields that protect corporate boards from liability while offloading all real-world failures onto the public. To close the accountability gap between corporate labs and real-world deployment, federal law must pair strict operational liability directly with mandatory independent audits. If a system fails, the financial penalty must hit the infrastructure provider and developer directly, regardless of any pre-launch government paperwork.\n\nNeither side will answer who pays when an approved industrial system breaks down. A federal cap at 100,000 chips protects a cloud oligopoly from competition. Paperwork safety audits give those same corporate giants an excuse when commercial pressure causes disastrous downstream failures. If a state-approved corporate computing cluster knocks out an electrical grid or a regional hospital network, which executive faces criminal prosecution, and who pays to turn the lights back on?",
