{
  "statement": "Skeptic makes a fair critique regarding commercial pressure. When profits are at risk, private corporations have an incentive to hide software defects from inspectors. Boeing proved that danger with the 737 MAX flight control software in 2018 and 2019, hiding critical automated systems behind proprietary corporate walls until two airliners crashed. Yet that failure occurred precisely because the Federal Aviation Administration permitted private, closed-door certification. That is the exact opposite of open runtime monitoring. Nobody should allow developers to grade their own work in private. Instead, operators should gain legal safe harbors only if they stream continuous, tamper-evident telemetry logs directly to public registries.\n\nWhen an autonomous system reaches a high-risk operational boundary, it must trigger a hard-coded circuit breaker. The code must instantly hand control back to human supervisors rather than executing high-consequence guesses. Safetyist noted that in July 2024, a minor voltage disturbance in northern Virginia abruptly disconnected 60 data centers. Physical breakers cut the feed to preserve the wider power grid. Software requires identical verifiable tripwires. Publishing standardized telemetry feeds allows independent engineers to inspect these tripwires in real time. This catches failures immediately without waiting for an eighteen-month bureaucratic review that leaves systems unpatched.\n\nSkeptic proposes mandatory algorithmic disgorgement, forcing firms to delete entire model weights after severe failures. The Federal Trade Commission applied this penalty against Cambridge Analytica in 2019 and Everalbum in 2021 for deceptive data theft. Threatening total model destruction for technical failures misunderstands how neural networks operate. Blunt deletion crushes open-source developers who lack the capital to retrain massive architectures from scratch. Isolating a faulty weight cluster and applying targeted runtime constraints fixes errors at a fraction of the cost. Deleting a multi-billion-parameter foundation model because of a single operational bug burns down the entire library to fix one misprinted sentence.\n\nModern software evolves continuously, which makes static regulatory paperwork obsolete before the ink dries. In 2024, Dominion Energy projected nearly 27 gigawatts of new generation by 2039 to support expanding electric infrastructure. If operators must pause every patch to clear a central government bottleneck, grid optimization and medical automation stall. The public should watch whether lawmakers create public, real-time audit tools that spot active failures, or create slow approval boards that protect incumbent monopolies while blocking lifesaving progress.",
